[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
The sysmanage agent runs as the unprivileged "sysmanage-agent" account and
escalates through the sudoers policy installed at

    %%PREFIX%%/etc/sudoers.d/sysmanage-agent

READ THAT FILE.  It is not a security boundary.  It grants install(1),
chmod(1), chown(8), sed(1) and sysrc(8) with unrestricted arguments, each of
which is by itself equivalent to root: "sudo chmod 4755 /bin/sh" and "sudo
install" of a file into sudoers.d are both trivially root.  This is inherent to
what the agent does -- it installs packages, deploys arbitrary files, manages
services and reboots the host -- and no binary allow-list can confine it.

What the unprivileged deployment does give you is ACCOUNTABILITY: sudo logs
every privileged action, whereas an agent running as root logs nothing.  Treat
the account as root-equivalent when you reason about the host.

The policy is a superset of what most sites need.  The air-gap (xorriso,
growisofs, mount, umount), repository-mirroring (rsync, createrepo_c, trickle)
and firewall (pfctl, ipfw) grants matter only if you use those features; delete
the lines you do not need and re-check the file:

    visudo -c -f %%PREFIX%%/etc/sudoers.d/sysmanage-agent

Always re-check after editing.  sudo discards a policy file containing a single
malformed line, and the agent then loses every grant at once.

To run the agent privileged instead, in which case the policy is never
consulted:

    sysrc sysmanage_agent_user="root"
    service sysmanage-agent restart

The account is deliberately NOT the "sysmanage" account used by the sysutils/
sysmanage server port.  That one runs a network-facing web application, and
sharing an account would let a web compromise inherit these grants.
EOM
}
]
